.TH stty "" "Command"
.SH NAME
stty \- set/print terminal modes
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBstty [\-t\fIttyname\fB] [\fImode ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
stty -- set/print terminal modes
Usage:	stty [ option ... ]
Options:
9600		Baud rate--any valid terminal speed accepted
0		Hang up phone immediately
[break | eof | erase | interrupt | kill | quit | start | stop] c
		Set specified character to given 'c'
cbreak	-cbreak	Enable (disable) break after every input character
crt	-crt	Enable (disable) crt character erasing
echo	-echo	Enable (disable) input character echoing
even	-even	Enable (disable) even parity
excl	-excl	Enable/disable exclusive use
flush	-flush	Flush/do not flush input queues
hup	-hup	Enable (disable) hang up on last close
nl	-nl	Disable (enable) newline mapping
odd	-odd	Enable (disable) odd parity
print		Print terminal attributes
raw	-raw	Enable/disable raw mode (also 'cooked', 'rawin', 'rawout')
tabs	-tabs	Disable (enable) tab expansion
tandem	-tandem	Enable (disable) tandem (start/stop) mode
.HE
With no
.I mode
arguments,
.B stty
prints the erase and kill characters, the special characters, the
line speed, and the enabled modes of the standard output.
With
.I mode
arguments,
.B stty
sets each requested mode, character, or speed, and applies them all
at once after the whole command line has been parsed.
.PP
Each
.I mode
argument is one of the following.
Any mode may be preceded by
.B \-
to select its opposite, except where noted.
.IP \fInumber\fR 1.3i
Set both the input and output line speed to
.IR number ,
a recognized baud rate:
.BR 0 ", " 50 ", " 75 ", " 110 ", " 134.5 ", " 150 ", " 200 ", " 300 ,
.BR 600 ", " 1200 ", " 1800 ", " 2000 ", " 2400 ", " 3600 ", " 4800 ,
.BR 7200 ", " 9600 ", " 19200 ", " exta ", or " extb .
A speed of
.B 0
hangs up the phone (drops modem control) immediately.
.IP \fBeven\fR
Use even parity.
.IP \fBodd\fR
Use odd parity.
.IP "\fBraw\fR, \fB\-raw\fR (also \fBcooked\fR)"
Enable, or disable, raw mode for both input and output: disable all
input and output character processing (including erase/kill editing,
signal characters, and newline mapping).
.IP \fBrawin\fR
Raw mode for input only.
.IP \fBrawout\fR
Raw mode for output only.
.IP \fBcbreak\fR
Break out of a read after every input character (character-at-a-time
input) instead of waiting for a full line.
.IP \fBnl\fR
Disable mapping of carriage return to newline on input.
.B \-nl
restores the mapping (the default).
.IP \fBecho\fR
Echo input characters as they are typed.
.IP \fBlcase\fR
Map upper-case input to lower case, for terminals that cannot
generate lower case.
.IP \fBtabs\fR
Disable expansion of tabs to spaces on output.
.B \-tabs
expands them (the default).
.IP \fBtandem\fR
Enable tandem mode: start and stop output in response to the
.B start
and
.B stop
characters.
.IP \fBcrt\fR
Enable erase and kill processing suited to a CRT terminal (which can
back up and overwrite) rather than a hardcopy terminal.
.IP \fBek\fR
Reset the erase and kill characters to
.B #
and
.BR @ ,
respectively.
.IP "\fBerase \fIc\fR"
Set the character that erases the last-typed character to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBkill \fIc\fR"
Set the character that erases the current input line to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBstart \fIc\fR"
Set the character that resumes output to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBstop \fIc\fR"
Set the character that suspends output to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBeof \fIc\fR"
Set the character that signals end of input to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBbreak \fIc\fR"
Set the alternate line-break character to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBquit \fIc\fR"
Set the character that raises a quit signal to
.IR c .
.IP "\fBint \fIc\fR"
Set the character that raises an interrupt signal to
.IR c .
.IP \fBhup\fR
Hang up the line when the last process that has the terminal open
closes it.
.IP \fBexcl\fR
Reserve the terminal for exclusive use by this process; a later
.B open
by another process fails until it is closed.
.B \-excl
clears the reservation.
.IP \fBflush\fR
When the modes given on this command line are applied, flush any
pending input first. This is the default.
.B \-flush
applies the modes without flushing pending input.
.IP \fBprint\fR
Print the terminal's attributes (as with no arguments), after
applying any other modes given on the same command line.
.PP
Each character
.I c
above may be given as a single literal character; as the word
.BR DEL ;
as
.BI \e ooo,
an octal escape (leading backslash, up to three octal digits); as
.BI ^ x
or
.BI ctrl\- x,
a control character; or as the word
.BR off ,
which disables that character (no character will trigger the
action).
.PP
An option
.BI \-t ttyname
names the terminal
.B /dev/\fIttyname\fR
to operate on, instead of the standard output; if given, it must be
the first argument.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B stty
complains if the standard output (or the terminal named by
.BR \-t )
is not a terminal, if a
.I mode
word is not recognized, or if a character specification is badly
formed.
.SH NOTES
Whether a given mode has any effect depends on the terminal driver in
use; requesting an unsupported mode is not itself an error.
